  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 PM CDT Sun Jun 21 2026\n\nAn upper shortwave trough is tracking east out of the Central \nRockies into the Central Plains with a surface low in northwestern \nOklahoma and a cold front extending from this low into central KS. \nFor later this afternoon into early tonight, storm development is \nexpected along the front as it continues to slowly push south with \nthe main chances in southeast KS and portions of our southern most \ncounties. Instability will increase from diurnal heating and \ndewpoints rising into the mid to upper 70s, resulting in MUCAPE \nvalues between 3000-3500 J/kg. This instability combined with 40 kts \nof effective shear, DCAPE values up to 1200 J/kg, and mid-level \nlapse rates between 7-7.5 C/km will be sufficient for severe storms \nto develop with damaging winds, large hail, and heavy rainfall as \nthe primary hazards. A brief landspout tornado can't be ruled out \nthis evening as low-level wind shear and 0-3km CAPE increases, \nthough chances remain very low. These storm chances will decrease \ninto the early overnight hours as activity shifts south into \nOklahoma. There is a low chance for storms to clip the western \nfringes of our area tonight as storms roll off the High Plains into \nwestern Kansas but the better chances should stay west of the area. \n\nDrier conditions are expected as we move into Monday morning and \nthroughout the day Monday. A shortwave trough is progged to move \ninto the Northern Plains Monday night with a weaker wave moving into \nthe Central and Southern Rockies which will initiate another round \nof storms Monday night into Tuesday morning. There is a very low \nchance this activity impacts southern KS with the better chances \nremaining south in Oklahoma. Storm chances will be possible on \nTuesday afternoon with about a 30% chance for areas along and west \nof I-135 as storms dive south out of Nebraska. Better chances will \narrive Tuesday night into Wednesday morning as a stronger shortwave \nslides across the Northern Plains with a LLJ anticipated to \nincrease across the Southern Plains into Kansas. Shower and \nstorm chances are expected to decrease throughout the day on \nWednesday. \n\nThis active pattern will continue through Friday as flow aloft \ncontinues to be westerly to northwesterly over the Central Plains \nwith embedded shortwaves progged to slide across the region. This \nwill result in storms rolling off the High Plains into our area with \nthe highest chances in the evenings into the overnight hours, \nkeeping periodic rain chances through much of the week. For \ntemperatures, highs are forecast to be in the low to mid 80s through \nmuch of the work week which is about 5-10 degrees below normal for \nthis time of year. A pattern chance is expected this weekend as \nupper ridging moves into the Plains with a deep upper trough digging \ninto the Northern Intermountain. Thickness will increase aiding\nin warmer temperatures with highs likely to rise into the 90s \nover the weekend.",
